srv: take the library's read lock before Context.lock

Context.search(), Context.get_categories() and Context.get_tag_browser()
take Context.lock and then call into the library, which takes its read
lock. get_books (srv/code.py) and ajax search (srv/ajax.py) hold the read
lock and then call Context.search(), which takes Context.lock. Two orders
for one pair of locks. Once a writer is queued, SHLock makes new readers
wait behind it, and three threads wait on each other for good:

  - the tag browser holds Context.lock and waits for the read lock;
  - a write waits for the write lock;
  - a page of books holds the read lock and waits for Context.lock.

The tag browser's generate() runs on the server's event-loop thread
(etagged_dynamic_response), so the whole server stops: no request is
served and SIGTERM is ignored. It shows within 15 s at 32 clients with 5%
writes.

This takes db.safe_read_lock before Context.lock in all three methods, so
there is one order everywhere. The read lock is reentrant for a thread that
already holds it, even with a writer queued, so callers that hold it are
unaffected, and safe_read_lock does nothing for a thread that holds the
write lock. get_categories() was already called under the read lock by
ajax.py, so its InvalidLinkTable repair path behaves as it does there.

Separately, and not done here: the tag browser's generate() should not run
on the event-loop thread. Any wait there stalls the whole server.

calibre's srv tests pass (50 run, 0 failures). A forced-interleaving test
that reproduces the deadlock (calibre-zen's src/calibre_zen/tests/test_srvfix.py)
passes with this change.

